1
00:00:00,338 --> 00:00:03,338
part 1 chapter 2.

2
00:00:03,338 --> 00:00:07,659
As he put his hand to the doorknob Winston saw that he had left the diary

3
00:00:07,659 --> 00:00:09,400
open on the table.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,599
DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER was written all over it, in letters almost big enough to

5
00:00:13,599 --> 00:00:15,460
be legible across the room.

6
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:18,699
It was an inconceivably stupid thing to have done.

7
00:00:18,699 --> 00:00:23,500
But, he realized even in Hispanic he had not wanted to smudge the creamy paper by

8
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,679
shutting the book while the ink was wet.

9
00:00:26,679 --> 00:00:32,858
He drew in his breath and opened the door. Instantly a warm wave of relief flowed through him.

10
00:00:32,858 --> 00:00:40,059
A colorless crushed looking woman, with wispy hair and a lion face, was standing outside.

11
00:00:40,059 --> 00:00:45,760
'Oh, comrade,' she began in a dreary, whining sort of voice, 'I thought I heard you come

12
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:50,500
in. Do you think you could come across and have a look at our kitchen sink. It's

13
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:56,739
got blocked up and— It was Mrs Parsons, the wife of a neighbor on the same floor.

14
00:00:56,739 --> 00:01:01,119
('Mrs' was a word somewhat discontinenced by the Party he was supposed to call

15
00:01:01,119 --> 00:01:05,559
everyone 'comrade'— '—but with some women one used it instinctively.)

16
00:01:05,559 --> 00:01:09,219
She was a woman of about 30 but looking much older.

17
00:01:09,219 --> 00:01:13,119
One had the impression that there was dust in the creases of her face.

18
00:01:13,119 --> 00:01:15,460
Winston followed her down the passage.

19
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:19,420
These amateur repair jobs were an almost daily irritation.

20
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:25,358
Victory Mansions were old flats, built in 1930 or thereabouts, and were falling to pieces.

21
00:01:25,358 --> 00:01:29,440
The plaster flaked constantly from ceilings and walls, the pipes burst in

22
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:33,219
every hard frost, the roof leaked whenever there was snow, the heating

23
00:01:33,219 --> 00:01:36,820
system was usually running at half steam when it was not closed down altogether

24
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:38,679
from motives of economy.

25
00:01:38,679 --> 00:01:42,820
Repairs, except what you could do for yourself, had to be sanctioned by remote

26
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:48,460
committees which were liable to hold up even the mending of a window pane for two years.

27
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:53,739
'Of course it's only because Tom isn't home,' said Mrs Parsons vaguely.

28
00:01:53,739 --> 00:01:57,820
The Parsons' flat was bigger than Winston's, and dingy in a different way.

29
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:02,019
Everything had a battered trampled on look, as though the place had just been

30
00:02:02,019 --> 00:02:04,780
visited by some large violent animal.

31
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:10,599
Games impedimenter hockey sticks, boxing gloves, a burst football, a pair of sweaty

32
00:02:10,599 --> 00:02:14,800
shorts turned inside out— —lay all over the floor, and on the table there was a

33
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:20,440
litter of dirty dishes and dog-eared exercise books On the walls were scarlet banners of the

34
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:25,239
Youth League and the Spies, and a full-sized poster of Big Brother.

35
00:02:25,239 --> 00:02:29,559
There was the usual boiled cabbage smell, common to the whole building, but it was

36
00:02:29,559 --> 00:02:34,239
shot through by a sharper reek of sweat, which— —one knew this at the first sniff,

37
00:02:34,239 --> 00:02:39,639
though it was hard to say how— —was the sweat of some person not present at the moment.

38
00:02:39,639 --> 00:02:43,659
In another room someone with a comb and a piece of toilet paper was trying to

39
00:02:43,659 --> 00:02:48,400
keep tuned with the military music which was still issuing from the telescreen.

40
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:53,139
'It's the children,' said Mrs Parsons, casting a half apprehensive glance at the door.

41
00:02:53,139 --> 00:02:58,780
'They haven't been out today. And of course———' She had a habit of breaking off her

42
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:00,338
sentences in the middle.

43
00:03:00,338 --> 00:03:04,599
The kitchen sink was full nearly to the brim with filthy greenish water which

44
00:03:04,599 --> 00:03:07,300
smelled worse than ever of cabbage.

45
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:12,519
Winston knelt down and examined the angle joint of the pipe. He hated using

46
00:03:12,519 --> 00:03:17,920
his hands, and he hated bending down, which was always liable to start him coughing.

47
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:20,800
Mrs Parsons looked on helplessly.

48
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:25,420
'Of course if Tom was home he'd put it right in a moment,' she said. 'He loves

49
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:30,039
anything like that. He's ever so good with his hands Tommy's

50
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:34,780
Parsons was Winston's fellow employee at the Ministry of Truth. He was a fetish

51
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:40,119
but active man of paralyzing stupidity, a massive imbecile enthusiasms— —one of

52
00:03:40,119 --> 00:03:44,679
those completely unquestioning, devoted drudgers on whom, more even than on the

53
00:03:44,679 --> 00:03:48,159
Thought Police, the stability of the Party depended.

54
00:03:48,159 --> 00:03:52,599
At 35 he had just been unwillingly evicted from the Youth League, and before

55
00:03:52,599 --> 00:03:56,858
graduating into the Youth League he had managed to stay on in the Spies for a

56
00:03:56,858 --> 00:03:59,320
year beyond the statutory age.

57
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:03,400
At the Ministry he was employed in some subordinate post for which intelligence

58
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:07,059
was not required, but on the other hand he was a leading figure on the Sports

59
00:04:07,059 --> 00:04:10,980
Committee and all the other committees engaged in organizing community hikes,

60
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:16,780
spontaneous demonstrations, savings campaigns and voluntary activities generally.

61
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:21,460
He would inform you with quiet pride, between whiffs on his pipe, that he had

62
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:26,380
put in an appearance at the Community center every evening for the past four years.

63
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:30,460
An overpowering smell of sweat, a sort of unconscious testimony to the

64
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:34,838
strenuousness of his life, followed him about wherever he went, and even remained

65
00:04:34,838 --> 00:04:38,019
behind him after he had gone.

66
00:04:38,019 --> 00:04:42,400
'Have you got a spanner?' said Winston, fiddling with the nut on the angle joint

67
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,659
A spanner,' said Mrs Parsons, immediately becoming invertebrate.

68
00:04:46,659 --> 00:04:52,358
'I don't know, I'm sure. Perhaps the children— There was a trampling of boots and

69
00:04:52,358 --> 00:04:56,559
another blast on the comb as the children charged into the living room

70
00:04:56,559 --> 00:05:01,300
Mrs Parsons brought the spanner. Winston let out the water and disgustedly

71
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:04,539
removed the clot of human hair that had blocked up the pipe.

72
00:05:04,539 --> 00:05:08,139
He cleaned his fingers as best he could in the cold water from the tap and went

73
00:05:08,139 --> 00:05:10,539
back into the other room.

74
00:05:10,539 --> 00:05:14,199
'Up with your hands!' yelled a savage voice.

75
00:05:14,199 --> 00:05:18,099
A handsome tough looking boy of nine had popped up from behind the table and was

76
00:05:18,099 --> 00:05:23,019
menacing him with a toy automatic pistol, while his small sister, about two years

77
00:05:23,019 --> 00:05:27,099
younger, made the same gesture with a fragment of wood.

78
00:05:27,099 --> 00:05:31,059
Both of them were dressed in the blue shorts gray shirts and a rednecker

79
00:05:31,059 --> 00:05:34,239
Chiefs which were the uniform of the Spies.

80
00:05:34,239 --> 00:05:38,739
Winston raised his hands above his head, but with an uneasy feeling, so vicious

81
00:05:38,739 --> 00:05:42,880
was the boy's demeanor that it was not altogether a game.

82
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:48,099
'You're a traitor!' yelled the boy. 'You're a thought criminal You're a Eurasian spy!

83
00:05:48,099 --> 00:05:53,079
I'll shoot you, I'll vaporize you, I'll send you to the salt mines!'

84
00:05:53,079 --> 00:05:56,739
Suddenly they were both leaping around him, shouting 'Traitor!' and thought

85
00:05:56,739 --> 00:06:00,820
criminal the little girl imitating her brother in every movement.

86
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:05,079
It was somehow slightly frightening, like the gambling of tiger cubs which will

87
00:06:05,079 --> 00:06:07,420
soon grow up into man-eaters.

88
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:11,920
There was a sort of calculating ferocity in the boy's eye, a quite evident desire

89
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:16,900
to hit or kick Winston and a consciousness of being very nearly big enough to do so.

90
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:22,059
It was a good job it was not a real pistol he was holding, Winston thought.

91
00:06:22,059 --> 00:06:26,858
Mrs Parsons eyes flitted nervously from Winston to the children, and back again.

92
00:06:26,858 --> 00:06:29,858
In the better light of the living room he noticed with interest that there

93
00:06:29,858 --> 00:06:33,780
actually was dust in the creases of her face.

94
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:38,320
'They do get so noisy,' she said. 'They're disappointed because they couldn't go to

95
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:42,400
see the hanging, that's what it is. I'm too busy to take them, and Tom won't be

96
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:44,019
back from work in time.'

97
00:06:44,019 --> 00:06:46,300
'Why can't we go and see the hanging?'

98
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:49,239
roared the boy in his huge voice.

99
00:06:49,239 --> 00:06:53,500
'Want to see the hanging! Want to see the hanging!' chanted the little girl, still

100
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:55,719
capering round.

101
00:06:55,719 --> 00:06:59,800
Some Eurasian prisoners, guilty of war crimes, were to be hanged in the Park

102
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:02,079
that evening, Winston remembered.

103
00:07:02,079 --> 00:07:05,858
This happened about once a month, and was a popular spectacle.

104
00:07:05,858 --> 00:07:09,280
Children always clamored to be taken to see it.

105
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:12,579
He took his leave of Mrs Parsons and made for the door.

106
00:07:12,579 --> 00:07:16,119
But he had not gone six steps down the passage when something hit the back of

107
00:07:16,119 --> 00:07:18,699
his neck and agonizingly painful blow.

108
00:07:18,699 --> 00:07:22,420
It was as though a red hot wire had been jabbed into him.

109
00:07:22,420 --> 00:07:26,380
He spun round just in time to see Mrs Parsons dragging her son back into the

110
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:34,838
doorway while the boy pocketed a catapult Gold's sign Billard the boy as the door closed on him

111
00:07:34,838 --> 00:07:40,980
utmost struck Winston was the look of helpless fright on the woman's grayish face.

112
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:45,400
Back in the flat he stepped quickly past the telescreen and sat down at the table

113
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,500
again, still rubbing his neck.

114
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:51,699
The music from the telescreen had stopped. Instead, a clipped military voice

115
00:07:51,699 --> 00:07:55,900
was reading out, with a sort of brutal relish, a description of the armaments of

116
00:07:55,900 --> 00:08:02,619
the new Floating Fortress which had just been anchored between Iceland and the Faroe Islands.

117
00:08:02,619 --> 00:08:07,358
With those children, he thought, that wretched woman must lead a life of terror.

118
00:08:07,358 --> 00:08:11,199
Another year, two years, and they would be watching her night and day for symptoms

119
00:08:11,199 --> 00:08:12,818
of unorthodoxy.

120
00:08:12,818 --> 00:08:15,699
Nearly all children nowadays were horrible.

121
00:08:15,699 --> 00:08:19,719
What was the worst of all was that by means of such organizations as the Spies

122
00:08:19,719 --> 00:08:24,400
they were systematically turned into ungovernable little savages, and yet this

123
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:29,019
produced in them no tendency whatever to rebel against the discipline of the Party.

124
00:08:29,019 --> 00:08:32,800
On the contrary, they adored the Party and everything connected with it.

125
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:37,239
The songs, the processions, the banners, the hiking, the drilling with dummy

126
00:08:37,239 --> 00:08:41,559
rifles, the yelling of slogans, the worship of Big Brother—

127
00:08:41,559 --> 00:08:44,739
—it was all a sort of glorious game to them.

128
00:08:44,739 --> 00:08:49,300
All their ferocity was turned outwards, against the enemies of the State, against

129
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:55,358
foreigners, traitors, saboteurs thought criminals It was almost normal for people over 30

130
00:08:55,358 --> 00:08:57,699
to be frightened of their own children.

131
00:08:57,699 --> 00:09:01,838
And with good reason, for hardly a weak past in which the Times did not carry a

132
00:09:01,838 --> 00:09:06,880
paragraph describing how some evils dropping little sneak—' —'child hero' was the

133
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:11,559
phrase generally used— —had overheard some compromising remark and denounced its

134
00:09:11,559 --> 00:09:13,900
parents to the thoughts Police.

135
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:17,800
The sting of the catapult bullet had worn off. He picked up his pen

136
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:22,179
half-heartedly, wondering whether he could find something more to write in the diary.

137
00:09:22,179 --> 00:09:25,960
Suddenly he began thinking of O'Brien again.

138
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:30,639
Years ago— —how long was it? Seven years it must be— —he had dreamed that he was

139
00:09:30,639 --> 00:09:32,619
walking through a pitch dark room.

140
00:09:32,619 --> 00:09:36,039
And someone sitting to one side of him had said as he passed:

141
00:09:36,039 --> 00:09:39,400
'We shall meet in the place where there is no darkness.'

142
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:44,199
It was said very quietly, almost casually— —a statement, not a command.

143
00:09:44,199 --> 00:09:46,659
He had walked on without pausing.

144
00:09:46,659 --> 00:09:50,800
What was curious was that at the time, in the dream, the words had not made much

145
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:52,420
impression on him.

146
00:09:52,420 --> 00:09:56,920
It was only later and by degrees that they had seemed to take on significance.

147
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:01,239
He could not now remember whether it was before or after having the dream that he

148
00:10:01,239 --> 00:10:05,440
had seen O'Brien for the first time; nor could he remember when he had first

149
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:08,199
identified the voice as O'Brien's.

150
00:10:08,199 --> 00:10:12,639
But at any rate the identification existed. It was O'Brien who had spoken to

151
00:10:12,639 --> 00:10:15,480
him out of the dark.

152
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,260
Winston had never been able to feel sure— —even after this morning's flash of the

153
00:10:20,260 --> 00:10:26,019
eyes it was still impossible to be sure— —whether O'Brien was a friend or an enemy.

154
00:10:26,019 --> 00:10:28,719
Nor did it even seem to matter greatly.

155
00:10:28,719 --> 00:10:34,480
There was a link of understanding between them, more important than affection or partisanship.

156
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:38,739
'We shall meet in the place where there is no darkness,' he had said.

157
00:10:38,739 --> 00:10:44,858
Winston did not know what it meant, only that in some way or another it would come true.

158
00:10:44,858 --> 00:10:47,559
The voice from the telescreen paused.

159
00:10:47,559 --> 00:10:51,579
A trumpet call, clear and beautiful, floated into the stagnant air.

160
00:10:51,579 --> 00:10:57,519
The voice continued raspingly: 'Attention! Your attention, please! A news

161
00:10:57,519 --> 00:11:03,099
flash has this moment arrived from the Malabar front uh forces in South India have won a

162
00:11:03,099 --> 00:11:07,780
glorious victory. I am authorized to say that the action we are now reporting may

163
00:11:07,780 --> 00:11:10,838
well bring the war within measurable distance of its end.

164
00:11:10,838 --> 00:11:15,880
Here is the news flash Bad news coming, thought Winston.

165
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:19,358
And sure enough, following on a gory description of the annihilation of a

166
00:11:19,358 --> 00:11:23,739
Eurasian army, with stupendous figures of killed and prisoners, came the

167
00:11:23,739 --> 00:11:30,159
announcement that, as from next week, the chocolate ration would be reduced from 30 grams to 20.

168
00:11:30,159 --> 00:11:35,500
Winston belched again. The gin was wearing off, leaving a deflated feeling.

169
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:39,699
The telescreen— —perhaps to celebrate the victory, perhaps to drown the memory of

170
00:11:39,699 --> 00:11:44,318
the lost chocolate— —crashed into 'Oceania, 'tis for thee'.

171
00:11:44,318 --> 00:11:46,599
You were supposed to stand to attention.

172
00:11:46,599 --> 00:11:50,920
However, in his present position he was invisible.

173
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:55,659
'Oceania is for thee' gave way to lighter music. Winston walked over to the window,

174
00:11:55,659 --> 00:11:58,300
keeping his back to the telescreen.

175
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:00,880
The day was still cold and clear.

176
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:05,739
Somewhere far away a rocket bomb exploded with a dull, reverberating roar.

177
00:12:05,739 --> 00:12:10,780
About 20 or 30 of them a week were falling on London at present.

178
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:14,980
Down in the street the wind flapped the torn poster to and fro, and the word

179
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:22,239
ingsock fitfully appeared and vanished insock The sacred principles of insock

180
00:12:22,239 --> 00:12:25,838
Newspeak, doublethink, the mutability of the past.

181
00:12:25,838 --> 00:12:29,920
He felt as though he were wandering in the forests of the sea bottom, lost in a

182
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:33,099
monstrous world where he himself was the monster.

183
00:12:33,099 --> 00:12:37,960
He was alone. The past was dead, the future was unimaginable.

184
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:42,639
What certainty had he that a single human creature now living was on his side?

185
00:12:42,639 --> 00:12:47,380
And what way of knowing that the dominion of the Party would not endure forever

186
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:51,280
Like an answer, the three slogans on the white face of the Ministry of Truth came

187
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:58,838
back to him: WAR IS PEACE FREEDOM IS SLAVERY IGNORANCE IS STRENGTH.

188
00:12:58,838 --> 00:13:01,719
He took a 25 cent piece out of his pocket.

189
00:13:01,719 --> 00:13:06,159
There, too, in tiny clear lettering, the same slogans were inscribed, and on the

190
00:13:06,159 --> 00:13:08,800
other face of the coin the head of Big Brother.

191
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:11,619
Even from the coin the eyes pursued you.

192
00:13:11,619 --> 00:13:17,199
On coins, on stamps, on the covers of books, on banners, on posters and on the

193
00:13:17,199 --> 00:13:20,380
wrappings of a cigarette packet— —everywhere.

194
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:24,159
Always the eyes watching you and the voice enveloping you

195
00:13:24,159 --> 00:13:29,739
sleep or awake, working or eating, indoors or out of doors, in the bath or in bed— —no

196
00:13:29,739 --> 00:13:30,880
escape.

197
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:36,239
Nothing was your own except the few cubic centimeters inside your skull.

198
00:13:36,239 --> 00:13:40,659
The sun had shifted round, and the myriad windows of the Ministry of Truth, with

199
00:13:40,659 --> 00:13:45,579
the light no longer shining on them, looked grim as the loopholes of a fortress.

200
00:13:45,579 --> 00:13:49,358
His heart quailed before the enormous pyramidal shape.

201
00:13:49,358 --> 00:13:55,358
It was too strong, it could not be stormed. A thousand rocket bombs would not batter it down.

202
00:13:55,358 --> 00:13:58,539
He wanted again for whom he was writing the diary.

203
00:13:58,539 --> 00:14:03,219
For the future, for the past— —for an age that might be imaginary.

204
00:14:03,219 --> 00:14:06,639
And in front of him they lay not death but annihilation.

205
00:14:06,639 --> 00:14:12,460
The diary would be reduced to ashes and himself to Vapor Only the Thought Police would read what

206
00:14:12,460 --> 00:14:16,659
he had written, before they wiped it out of existence and out of memory.

207
00:14:16,659 --> 00:14:20,380
How could you make appeal to the future when not a trace of you, not even an

208
00:14:20,380 --> 00:14:25,858
anonymous word scribbled on a piece of paper, could physically survive?

209
00:14:26,019 --> 00:14:30,219
The telescreen struck 14. He must leave in 10 minutes.

210
00:14:30,219 --> 00:14:34,300
He had to be back at work by 1430.

211
00:14:34,300 --> 00:14:38,619
Curiously, the chiming of the ass seemed to have put new heart into him.

212
00:14:38,619 --> 00:14:43,179
He was a lonely ghost uttering a truth that nobody would ever hear.

213
00:14:43,179 --> 00:14:47,980
But so long as he uttered it, in some obscure way the continuity was not broken.

214
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:53,800
It was not by making yourself heard but by staying sane that you carried on the human heritage.

215
00:14:53,800 --> 00:14:57,460
He went back to the table, dipped his pen, and wrote:

216
00:14:57,460 --> 00:15:01,900
To the future or to the past, to a time when thought is free, when men are

217
00:15:01,900 --> 00:15:06,940
different from one another and do not live alone— —to a time when truth exists and what is

218
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:11,260
done cannot be undone: From the age of uniformity, from the age

219
00:15:11,260 --> 00:15:18,480
of solitude, from the age of Big Brother, from the age of doublethink— —greetings!

220
00:15:19,119 --> 00:15:23,920
He was already dead, he reflected seemed to him that it was only now, when

221
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:28,780
he had begun to be able to formulate his thoughts, that he had taken the decisive step.

222
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:32,440
The consequences of every act are included in the act itself.

223
00:15:32,440 --> 00:15:39,480
He wrote thought crime does not entail death thought crime IS death.

224
00:15:39,639 --> 00:15:44,079
Now he had recognized himself as a dead man it became important to stay alive as

225
00:15:44,079 --> 00:15:45,760
long as possible.

226
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:49,900
Two fingers of his right hand were extend It was exactly the kind of detail

227
00:15:49,900 --> 00:15:51,579
that might betray you.

228
00:15:51,579 --> 00:15:56,139
Some nosing zealot in the Ministry (a woman, probably: someone like the little

229
00:15:56,139 --> 00:15:59,858
Sandy haired woman or the dark-haired girl from the Fiction Department)

230
00:15:59,858 --> 00:16:04,119
might start wondering why he had been writing during the lunch interval, why he

231
00:16:04,119 --> 00:16:08,139
had used an old-fashioned pen, what he had been writing—

232
00:16:08,139 --> 00:16:11,139
—and then drop a hint in the appropriate quarter.

233
00:16:11,139 --> 00:16:14,500
He went to the bathroom and carefully scrubbed the ink away with the gritty

234
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:18,940
dark brown soap, which rasped your skin like sandpaper and was therefore well

235
00:16:18,940 --> 00:16:21,818
adapted for this purpose.

236
00:16:21,818 --> 00:16:25,960
He put the diary away in the drawer. It was quite useless to think of hiding it,

237
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:30,880
but he could at least make sure whether or not its existence had been discovered.

238
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:34,179
A hair laid across the page ends was too obvious.

239
00:16:34,179 --> 00:16:37,900
With the tip of his finger he picked up an identifiable grain of whitish dust

240
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:42,039
and deposited it on the corner of the cover, where it was bound to be shaken

241
00:16:42,039 --> 00:16:44,000
off if the book was moved
